steps.body:
A strategy often does real work in its constructor: it subscribes to a feed, loads a volatility surface, or registers with the risk system. So a line that looks like it builds one, but doesn't, is a silent bug.

C++ follows one rule here: if a statement can be read as a declaration, it is one. *Constructors, Destructors, and RAII* showed that `Student s();` declares a function. The **most vexing parse** is the bigger version:

```cpp harness
#include <bits/stdc++.h>

struct Config {
    int depth = 5;
};

struct Engine {
    explicit Engine(Config c) : depth(c.depth) {
        std::cout << "engine started\n";
    }
    double price() const {
        return 100.0;
    }
    int depth;
};

int main() {
    {{code}}
    std::cout << "end of main\n";
    return 0;
}
```

```cpp
Engine engine(Config());  // a declaration: "engine started" never prints
```

This declares a function named `engine` that returns an `Engine` and takes one parameter: a function with no parameters that returns a `Config`. No `Engine` exists, so its constructor never runs. Call a member on it and the build fails with an error that names the real type:

```cpp harness
#include <bits/stdc++.h>

struct Config {
    int depth = 5;
};

struct Engine {
    explicit Engine(Config c) : depth(c.depth) {
        std::cout << "engine started\n";
    }
    double price() const {
        return 100.0;
    }
    int depth;
};

int main() {
    {{code}}
    std::cout << "end of main\n";
    return 0;
}
```

```cpp error
Engine engine(Config());
std::cout << engine.price() << '\n';  // error: request for member 'price' in 'engine', which is of non-class type 'Engine(Config (*)())'
```

The dangerous case is a line nothing uses by name, where the constructor's side effect was the point. Then nothing fails, and g++ 10.2 gives no warning even with `-Wall -Wextra`. The `-Wvexing-parse` warning only arrived in GCC 11.

Three fixes build a real `Engine`:

```cpp harness
#include <bits/stdc++.h>

struct Config {
    int depth = 5;
};

struct Engine {
    explicit Engine(Config c) : depth(c.depth) {
        std::cout << "engine started\n";
    }
    double price() const {
        return 100.0;
    }
    int depth;
};

int main() {
    {{code}}
    std::cout << "end of main\n";
    return 0;
}
```

```cpp
Engine a{Config{}};    // braces can't be a parameter list
Engine b((Config()));  // extra parentheses can't wrap a parameter
Config cfg;
Engine c(cfg);         // a named object, not a type
```

Braces are the everyday fix, and they also check narrowing.

